          "guid": "ms32cm9isoe9kd3j",
          "message": {
            "text": "The test that used to sit here is why this deserves a note: it would have survived the upgrade while becoming false.\n\nkerf 2.0 changed delegateCapture() from strict target.matches() to delegate()-style closest() walk-up, handing the handler the matched ancestor. The old test was named \"matches the target element directly (strict, no walk-up)\" but only asserted that a direct hit fires — true under both semantics. So a green suite would have reported that we'd verified strict matching we no longer had.\n\nThe replacements assert the discriminating cases: a click on a descendant now reports the ancestor, and { match: 'direct' } refuses that same descendant while still firing on the element itself.\n\nWorth knowing for the next kerf bump: no production code calls delegateCapture today, so this test file is the only thing pinning the behavior of a re-export we ship.\n",
            "markdown": "The test that used to sit here is why this deserves a note: it would have survived the upgrade while becoming false.\n\nkerf 2.0 changed `delegateCapture()` from strict `target.matches()` to `delegate()`-style `closest()` walk-up, handing the handler the matched ancestor. The old test was named \"matches the target element directly (strict, no walk-up)\" but only asserted that a *direct* hit fires — true under both semantics. So a green suite would have reported that we'd verified strict matching we no longer had.\n\nThe replacements assert the discriminating cases: a click on a descendant now reports the ancestor, and `{ match: 'direct' }` refuses that same descendant while still firing on the element itself.\n\nWorth knowing for the next kerf bump: no production code calls `delegateCapture` today, so this test file is the only thing pinning the behavior of a re-export we ship.\n"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/client/reactive.test.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 147,
                  "endLine": 190,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "  it('delegateCapture fires on the element itself', () => {\n    const root = document.createElement('div');\n    root.innerHTML = '<input class=\"field\" />';\n    document.body.appendChild(root);\n    let fired = 0;\n    const dispose = delegateCapture<HTMLInputElement>(root, 'focus', '.field', () => { fired += 1; });\n    root.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('.field')!.dispatchEvent(new FocusEvent('focus'));\n    expect(fired).toBe(1);\n    dispose();\n    root.remove();\n  });\n\n  // HS-9449 — kerf 2.0 changed `delegateCapture` from strict target matching to\n  // `delegate()`-style `closest()` walk-up, handing the handler the matched ANCESTOR\n  // rather than the raw target. The test that used to live here asserted only that a\n  // direct hit fires, which is true under both semantics — so it would have gone on\n  // passing while its name (\"strict, no walk-up\") became false. These two pin the\n  // actual behavior in both directions.\n  it('delegateCapture walks up to the matched ancestor by default (kerf 2.0 semantics)', () => {\n    const root = document.createElement('div');\n    root.innerHTML = '<div class=\"row\" data-idx=\"4\"><span class=\"inner\">x</span></div>';\n    document.body.appendChild(root);\n    let idx = '';\n    const dispose = delegateCapture<HTMLElement>(root, 'click', '.row', (_e, matched) => { idx = matched.dataset.idx ?? ''; });\n    root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.inner')!.click();\n    expect(idx).toBe('4'); // the ancestor, not the clicked <span>\n    dispose();\n    root.remove();\n  });\n\n  it('delegateCapture with { match: \"direct\" } restores strict target matching', () => {\n    const root = document.createElement('div');\n    root.innerHTML = '<div class=\"row\" data-idx=\"4\"><span class=\"inner\">x</span></div>';\n    document.body.appendChild(root);\n    let fired = 0;\n    const dispose = delegateCapture<HTMLElement>(root, 'click', '.row', () => { fired += 1; }, { match: 'direct' });\n    root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.inner')!.click(); // descendant — must NOT fire\n    expect(fired).toBe(0);\n    root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.row')!.click(); // the element itself — fires\n    expect(fired).toBe(1);\n    dispose();\n    root.remove();\n  });\n});"
                  }
                }
              }
            }
          ],
